            <ArticleTitle>[Complications in the hemiplegic patient in the first year after the stroke].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>206-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">After a cerebrovascular accident (CVA) a hemiplegic patient is at risk from numerous complications after leaving hospital.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To analyze the frequency of occurrence of the commonest complications characteristic of the hemiplegic syndrome, during the first year after the stroke.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We evaluated 73 hemiplegic patients admitted to the rehabilitation department for treatment after a stroke. At each medical consultation, on admission and three, six and twelve months after the CVA, the complications 'belonging' to the hemiplegic syndrome were evaluated. These included contractures, painful shoulder, sympathetic-reflex dystrophy, fractures and thalamic pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">During the first year of the illness 81% of the patients had some type of complication. A painful shoulder was the commonest complication, seen in 40% of the patients, followed by contractures (23%). Sympathetic-reflex dystrophy of the paretic arm occurred in 11 patients (15%).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The commonest complication in patients during the first year after their stroke are painful shoulder and contractures.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
